               {"text": "nainaṃ chindanti iti. enaṃ prakṛtaṃ dehinaṃ na chindanti śastrāṇi, niravayavatvāt na avayavavibhāgaṃ kurvanti. śastrāṇi asyādīni.tathā na enaṃ dahati pāvakaḥ, agniḥ api na bhasmīkaroti. tathā – na ca enaṃ kledayanti āpaḥ. apāṃ hi sāvayavasya vastunaḥ ārdrībhāvakaraṇena avayavaviśleṣāpādane sāmarthyam. tat na niravayave ātmani sambhavati. tathā snehavat dravyaṃ snehaśoṣaṇena nāśayati vāyuḥ. enaṃ tu ātmānaṃ na śoṣayati mārutaḥ api. yataḥ evaṃ tasmāt –", "tr": "'Weapons do not cut it…' Weapons — swords and the like — do not cut this embodied one under discussion; since it has no parts, they cannot divide it into parts. Likewise fire does not burn it, does not reduce it to ashes. Likewise water does not wet it: water has the power to separate the parts of a thing that has parts by making it moist, but this is not possible in the partless Self. Likewise the wind destroys a moist substance by drying up its moisture; but the wind does not dry up this Self. Since this is so —"},
