               {"text": "ātmanaḥ anityatvaṃ abhyupagamya idaṃ ucyate –", "intro": True, "tr": "Granting for argument's sake that the Self is impermanent, this is said:"},
               {"text": "atha cainamiti – “atha ca iti abhyupagamārthaḥ. enaṃ prakṛtam ātmānaṃ, nityajātaṃ, loka prasiddhyā pratyanekaśarīrotpattiṃ jātaḥ jātaḥ iti vā manyase, tathā pratitattadvināśaṃ nityaṃ vā manyase mṛtaṃ, mṛtaḥ mṛtaḥ iti, tathāpi tathā abhāve api ātmani tvaṃ mahābāho na evaṃ śocitum arhasi. janmavataḥ nāśaḥ nāśavataḥ janma ca ityetau avaśyambhāvinau iti. yasmāt –", "tr": "'And even if…' 'And even if' is used in the sense of granting. Even if you think of this Self as constantly born — born again and again with each of many bodies, as people commonly think — or think of it as constantly dying, dying again with the destruction of each body, even so, even if the Self were like that, mighty-armed one, you should not grieve like this; for destruction for what is born, and birth for what is destroyed, are both inevitable. Because —"},
