               {"text": "prasāda iti – prasāde sarvaduḥkhānām ādhyātmikādīnāṃ hāniḥ vināśaḥ asya yateḥ upajāyate. kiṃ ca prasannacetasaḥ svasthāntaḥkaraṇasya hi yasmāt āśu śīghraṃ buddhiḥ paryavatiṣṭhate, ākāśamiva parisamantāt, avatiṣṭhate, ātmasvarūpeṇaiva niścalībhavati ityarthaḥ. evaṃ prasannacetasaḥ avasthitabuddheḥ kṛtakṛtyatā yataḥ tasmāt rāgadveṣaviyuktaiḥ indriyaiḥ śāstrāviruddheṣu avarjanīyeṣu yuktaḥ samācaret ityarthaḥ.", "tr": "'In serenity…' When there is serenity, the end — the destruction — of all sorrows, those arising from the body and the rest, comes to this ascetic. Moreover, because the understanding of one whose mind is serene, whose inner organ is well, soon becomes firmly established — settles all around like space, becomes motionless in the very nature of the Self: that is the meaning. Since one whose mind is thus serene and whose understanding is settled has done all that is to be done, let the disciplined man engage, with senses free from attraction and aversion, in the unavoidable objects not forbidden by scripture — that is the meaning."},
